​1. System Requirements & Setup
​Prerequisites - ​Before running the simulator, ensure your system has the following software installed:
- ​Python 3.8 or higher
- ​Flask (pip install flask)
- ​Any modern web browser (Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, or Safari)

​Installation & Launch Instructions

1. Directory Structure - Ensure your project folder contains the following structure:
project-root/
│
├── app.py                   # Flask server wrapper
├── algorithm.py             # Core SRTF simulation backend
├── samplejobs.csv           # Preset job manifest dataset
└── interface/               # Frontend asset directory
    ├── index.html           # Structure and controls
    ├── style.css            # Custom CSS layout & theme
    └── script.js            # Animation ticker & UI controller

2. Start the Flask Server - Open a terminal or command prompt in your project root folder and execute:
python app.py

3. Access the Interface - 
Open your web browser and navigate to the link provided


2. Interface Overview

​The user interface is organized into six functional sections:
​Header - Displays the project title (Shortest Remaining Time First Simulator) and course description (CSP108 – Operating System Midterm Project).
​Job Input Section - Contains the input fields, dataset action buttons, and the Job Input Table displaying all registered processes.
​Controls Deck - Features execution controls (Start, Pause, Reset), an animation Speed selector, and a real-time Simulation Clock Ticker.
​Live Simulation Area:
- ​CPU Box - Shows the currently executing job ID and its dynamic remaining burst time.
- ​Ready Queue Container - Displays color-coded badges for all processes currently waiting in line.
​Event Log Section - Displays a scrolling, time-stamped text record of every process event (arrivals, dispatches, preemptions, and completions).
​Gantt Chart & Results Section - Renders a color-coded visual execution timeline alongside a summary table with comprehensive performance statistics.

​3. Step-by-Step Operating Guide

​Step 1: Loading or Entering Process Data
​You can populate jobs into the scheduling table using two methods:

​Option A: Loading Sample CSV Dataset
- ​Click the "Load Sample Jobs (CSV)" button.
- ​The system fetches samplejobs.csv via the backend API and populates the table automatically (e.g., J001: Climate Simulation, J002: Statistical Analysis, etc.).
​Option B: Manual Job Entry
​In the Job Input form, enter the required fields:
- ​Job ID: A unique text identifier (e.g., J008).
- ​Job Name: A descriptive process name (e.g., Database Query).
- ​Arrival Time: A non-negative whole number (e.g., 2).
- ​Estimated Duration: A positive whole number representing the CPU burst length (e.g., 8).
​Click "Add Job".
​Note: To reset the input list, click "Clear Jobs".

​Step 2: Configuring Simulation Controls
​Before launching the simulation:

​Select your desired animation playback speed from the Speed drop-down menu:
- ​Slow (1500ms per unit) - Best for step-by-step observation and classroom analysis.
- ​Normal (1000ms per unit) - Standard real-time animation rate.
- ​Fast (400ms per unit) - Recommended for large datasets with high burst times.

​Step 3: Running and Monitoring the Simulation
1. ​Click the "Start" button to launch the simulator.
2. ​Observe the live state components as the clock advances:
- ​Clock Ticker (Current Time) - Displays the current discrete time unit (T).
​- CPU Box - Turns dark and highlights the process ID actively utilizing the CPU, alongside its remaining time countdown.
- ​Ready Queue - Displays color-coded badges of waiting jobs, re-sorted automatically by shortest remaining time.
- ​Event Log - Appends live messages detailing state transitions (e.g., [T=3] J002 preempted J001, [T=8] J002 completed execution).
​- Gantt Chart - Progressively builds colored blocks for each executed time unit, automatically merging adjacent units belonging to the same job.

​Step 4: Pausing, Resuming, and Resetting
- ​Pause / Resume: Click the "Pause" button at any point during execution to freeze time and inspect current queue states or log entries. Click "Resume" to continue playback.
​- Reset Simulation: Click "Reset" to clear the current visual timeline, clock, and output results while keeping your inputted job list intact for re-execution.

​Step 5: Analyzing Results and Performance Metrics
​Once the clock reaches the final process completion time, the simulation stops automatically, displaying:
1. ​Process Results Table:
- ​Completion Time (CT)
- ​Turnaround Time (TT)
​Waiting Time (WT)
2. ​Global Analytics Dashboard:
​- Average Waiting Time (AWT)
- ​Average Turnaround Time (ATAT)
- ​Total Burst Time & Total Finish Time
- ​CPU Utilization (%)
- ​Throughput (Jobs/ms)
- ​Preemption Count

​4. Error Messages & Troubleshooting

Message / ErrorCauseSolution
"Job ID cannot be empty."Job ID field was left blank.Enter a valid string identifier (e.g., J001).
"Duplicate Job ID: [ID]"The Job ID entered already exists in the table.Provide a unique identifier for each job.
"Arrival time and duration must be whole numbers..."Inputs contained decimals, negative values, or letters.Use non-negative integers for Arrival Time (\ge 0) and positive integers for Duration (> 0).
"Add jobs or load the sample CSV first."Simulation started without any process entries.Add at least one valid job or click "Load Sample Jobs (CSV)".
